#import "../common_features.typ": *

Left once more to the winnowing silence of her cave, Kella of Aspenwood again burrows completely into their furs. The visit from the wayward queen was long enough to bring Kella into themselves, to begin to feel again the awakening of her mind to the truth of the material even in a sanctuary she carved out with the express purpose to escape that truth. 

These are the furs of creatures slain, if not by her hand then by the Extension of their will lived out in others' actions. Kolomaeri masters of craft cleaned the furs and lined them inside with the finest velvet. Much ado made about Kolomaer's weapons of war bearing the blessing of Enduring Quality. More impressive to Kella that the more tender craft of weaving is equally blessed: these furs of theirs cannot be soiled, and will remain clean and soft and warm until long after Kella's bones are dust. And yet, before the wonders of craft could bless them, first their material must be extracted, and the means to do that is violence. Hunt a creature for its fur. Use the rest or not, as you might. Capture another entirely for a quirk of something its body makes. Cultivate an entire category of being that lives within your domesticity and produces for you _silk_, which you extract with or without regard for the quiet, aching little lives of the beings who have been bent toward your will for production. What matters your care or tenderness for the lives that were taken? They have been taken beyond the veil, either of death or of servitude.

It is a difficult task, sometimes, to set all of that aside. Reduce down the history of the object to the effects of its material on your skin. Quiet an endless stream of thoughts before they can brush against the walls--walls you carved out after scaling a mountain while consumed by a fever that posed more threat to you than the torches and long knives of the frightened folk of Aspenwood, and Ea’river, and Qastra. It was not you who took their children. It was not you who--no. No.

Who is "you?" There is not a "you." There is only the darkness, and the comforting heat, and a surrounding so soft that even skin is forgotten. Abandon the thought of a body. Abandon the thought of a self. If there is a "you," then she is Veneth, as known by those who walk upon her. "You" are the land, the sea, the sky.

You are their angry congress, the storm turning in upon itself where the sea of Goletha assails the shores of Qastra.

You observe Persaval, where he stands in typical ostentatious opposition to your mounting wrath. He has closed himself to the touch of the Circle that claims him. You reflect on the rage that so clearly seethes within him, and its counterpart within your storm. Then you reflect on the Circle, and use that to consider the purpose of your observation.

The Circle is--or will be--restored. Mere has just bound herself again with Kella, that little waif hiding somewhere in her labyrinth of mountain caves. The Falcon God of Messengers now walks the Bridge that is sister to all worlds, where she seeks the kernel of love and yearning at the eye of Persaval's personal storm. Then her attention will turn to Kolomaer, just as Kella's thoughts had so recently turned, in order to draw poor Anshall back from the edge of his own reckoning with mortality.

You can return to the Eye in a moment. There are some things it is still worth taking note of elsewhere, first. Others cannot collect evidence at pace with you. You are, after all, a different kind of eye. All-seeing, when you want to be. And there is a question that you, like the Circle, would like to pose to Veneth.

"What will you do, now that you're no longer held by the throat?"

You think you might already know the answer. You feel it in the deepness of yourself, echoing out to parts that can report the anxiety. Anxiety, that most elemental of feelings. The first report of a nerve to some central authority, before ever there was hope that there could be an answer. Is there anything outside the self? Will the self continue? Am "I" going to be okay? It can all start to feel so small.

But you mustn't let your gaze drift too far out. There is beauty and horror to be found in the vast abyss of Being Without Self, but you are here with a goal to accomplish.

_Ea'river_. 

Her cities have not yet noticed their grand prophet's absence. Servants, Aligned or not, go about their appointed tasks with the same diligence as before. There is one exception you notice in Nailport, a city settled in one of the calmest of the Golethan bays. Within that port there is a single captain of the Qastran Legion, armed and armored with Kolomaeri steel that is as defiant to the weathering of time as his own body. This captain, Bendak, is embedded within the bureaucracy that maintains the safety of trade arriving at the port. Bendak, alerted by some tremor in his soul, looks up from a manifest and out into the fog of the sea. Straight at you. And while you, the Eye of All Things, are meeting many thousands of gazes at the moment this one seems to notice something _more_ about what he has locked eyes with.

His gaze stretches long across the sea of Goletha--to trace its arc is no matter to you--and lands on (or passes through) the lantern light of a ship of the Gentlefishers who sail untouched by the turbulence of the sea. That crew of many masters shirks the gaze of your alarmed Captain Bendak, but their collective Second pauses and flinches, as if he feels the look prick itself between his shoulder blades. The sudden stop rattles the golden chains dangling from his collar, turning the pause into a quiet little bell-song of concern that is quickly silenced. The Second does not turn toward the source of that sense. Instead his gaze also snaps toward _you_, watching the Second and the Captain as they peer at you with a qualm churning up in your collective stomachs.

You can trace that line, if you wish, to where you know it leads. Qastra, buried alive by its oaths, and the looming storm. But first...

Those far High Halls of Kolomaer have their own eyes peering out. Kolomaer alone retains records of its contracts with Lost Naris. Kolomaer alone, that nation where no craft may be permitted to pass away, refuses the pretence that Ea'river and Qastra long since accepted: that there was never a land of masked gods who served and guided the other nations in equal turn. Kolomaer alone, and the Circle bound to a Falcon and her Sparrow, who honor, as they can, their Sworn Sword's oath to remember what even Veneth has abandoned. Anshall's face, slumped and hardly meeting the eyes of his courtiers, does not turn to you, but it does occasionally slip out into the other fog, the one that shrouds the ruins of what was.

Lost Naris itself is barred to your sight. The will of that prophet, and the wills he bent towards the scouring that occurred, befouled the Marshall's Gates and blinded Veneth to all of Naris when they broke the countenance of the gods. But another lost nation, abandoned not only by Veneth but her own people, is easy for you to spy. The high stone walls of the island fortress Goletha stand empty. 

You complete your survey, and the question persists. There is an Accepted Model of Veneth held by scholars and heads of state across her breadth, and it is thus: as with the Paradigms, there are only ever _Five Nations_ of note. Some new nations may rise as others fall, so there may be some period of transition between them, but in the end the equilibrium of the world is for there to be a balance of the Five against each other. Thrast is _breaking_ that model. Goletha disappeared from the nations of the world long ago. After it did, Ea'river emerged as a new nation, birthed out of the Wilds with its wealth of amber and its love of history. Then Naris was struck down, a militant action by several of the Five that argued Naris was a threat to the balance of Veneth. But then no new nation emerged to take Naris' place.

Then Qastra faded, bleached white in the sun. Not really gone, but neither truly a nation any longer.

And yet Thrast flourished, Veneth persisted, and the expected cycle of emergence did not continue.

So the theory became, among those who could dare to debate the movements of the world from their corners, that Thrast must be somehow bending Veneth against her natural course, warping her into some new and terrible shape that would ultimately spell the end of Veneth as we know her. 

The Circle does not believe in the 'Accepted Model,' exactly. Or, that's not why you have risked everything and un-spooled yourself into the ether of all things in order to see what has happened now that Thrast has been cut off from Veneth. It's more personal than that for each of you. Or, for the rest of the Circle. You, Kella, have a hard time reconciling with the _personal_. You don't trust where your mind goes when you start thinking too long about what 'Kella' wants.

So you stay wrapped up in the comfort of life outside life and project yourself into Veneth.

But even then it's hard to retain that distance in the end. Can you be sure that the panic and loneliness you think you feel pinging out from Veneth herself is really the soul of your world, or is that your own, crying out from your own singular abyss?

If Thrast is really gone, and he was truly bending Veneth out of her natural shape, then why does Veneth feel like she's curling in on herself worse than ever? What if cutting Thrast out of the world is what kills Veneth, not saves her? What if that unspeakable dominance of his will upon the world was _everything that Veneth wanted_, not just another abuse he visits on the largest and most consequential of his victims?

Kella sends along the bond of the Circle to Mere that no, Veneth does not seem to be recovering in Thrast's absence. There are no signs of some new people and land clawing its way free from the Wilds. She reserves for herself her deeper fears, tucking them away into a quiet corner of her abyss, before becoming _you_ again.

You see Persaval facing the storm like it is a giant he means to slay. Dermike is in his hand, flickering occasionally with a pale unconscious flame. But Persaval's gaze is not so striking here. It's that Captain Bendak, and the Second, and the storm itself, and the company of awakened Legionnaires casting themselves hundreds of feet down from their cliff-side keep to land in sickening, reconstituting heaps just outside the glassy eye of Persaval's own _personal_ storm. But they're hardly looking at Persaval.

No, they're all looking at the boy who has emerged into the storm. The torn shirt, the sleek little black brick in his hand, and the look in his eyes as he peers into the storm. This is the boy Mere spoke of. This is David. _You_ cannot touch him. You become transfixed in your furs in your cave in the mountains. He, like you, watches all of this unfolding. But he cannot have the smallest measure of your context for it all. For him this is something else. You don't need to touch him to feel it. Your Extension reacts on its own. It wraps itself in the eddies of his wake.

All of Veneth feels like she is staring at David, and he is staring back, and you can see that what he is searching for and finding in the gaze that meets him is Thrast, in all his imperious wrath. You see a boy incredulous at a brand new world that hates him. 

He says, "Fine," and though the noise of his voice should be buried in the Cacophony of All Things it sounds like a whipcrack in your ears. He takes another step towards the storm, and the resolution on his face doesn't look like bravery. It looks like he's wrapping himself in furs and peeling himself apart for someone else's purpose.

You mutter to yourself aloud the things you spoke to Mere. Stricken, indeed.